WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.856 --> 00:00:03.050
East leads West by forty-three point

2
00:00:03.075 --> 00:00:06.051
seven million dollars in
won annual contract value.

3
00:00:06.659 --> 00:00:08.351
Should West copy East's approach?

4
00:00:08.664 --> 00:00:12.818
Start with the numbers, then test the
interpretation before making that decision.

5
00:00:14.637 --> 00:00:17.656
The first follow-up separates
deal count from deal size.

6
00:00:18.093 --> 00:00:21.990
About ninety-three percent of the gap
comes from East having more won deals.

7
00:00:22.104 --> 00:00:24.568
Average deal value explains only a small part.

8
00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:28.324
That describes the difference; it
does not yet explain the cause.

9
00:00:30.678 --> 00:00:32.627
The next question changes the reading.

10
00:00:32.982 --> 00:00:34.806
East has more indexed accounts,

11
00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:37.964
while West is slightly ahead
on won value per account.

12
00:00:38.468 --> 00:00:40.577
The account mixes differ, so the headline

13
00:00:40.602 --> 00:00:43.269
totals are not a fair measure
of team effectiveness.

14
00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:49.255
The recommendation is to hold the
rollout and build a matched comparison.

15
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.010
Sales operations checks opportunity supply,

16
00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:54.058
conversion, and account potential.

17
00:00:54.666 --> 00:00:57.727
A targeted pilot follows only
if the evidence supports it.

18
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.429
Better follow-up questions lead
to a more defensible decision.
